    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but isn't that kind of a losing game for the art itself? if we just accept that art is supposed to be a \"political intervention,\" aren't we basically just saying that art has lost its soul and is just becoming propaganda? it feels like the intrinsic value of art is being wiped out by these agendas.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The debate here is whether the \"soul\" of art is inherent in its form or in its critical impact, a divide that is very central to 20th and 21st-century discourse. To view it purely as propaganda dismisses the fact that powerful political critique often *is* the most profound and deeply \"sincere\" subject for an artist to tackle. The risk, of course, is that the strong political agenda can indeed become the *only* value, turning the work into partisan advocacy rather than a complex, human exploration.",
